Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can help. Before I forward anything: do you want me to send your medical details to vince2000@gmail.com with subject "User Medical Information" (that’s your preferred workflow in your settings)? If yes, I’ll send exactly what you wrote. If not, I’ll just help you prepare questions and a draft you can send to Dr. Wilson.

A few quick clarifying questions (so my suggestions are accurate):
- Can you confirm your current meds and doses (you listed apixaban 5 mg twice daily and metoprolol 25 mg — you also repeated metoprolol next to “anemia”; is there another med for anemia?)  
- What was the INR value? You wrote “320” — did you mean 3.20 or something else? (Note: INR monitoring is used for warfarin, not apixaban.)  
- Do you have a history of atrial fibrillation, prior clot, or another reason you’re on apixaban?  
- Any recent home BP readings (typical systolic/diastolic) or symptoms (dizziness, chest pain, palpitations, shortness of breath)?

If you want to proceed now, here are useful things to bring/ask at your annual physical and items to consider about a cardiology referral.

Suggested questions to ask Dr. James Wilson about hypertension management
- What should my target blood pressure be given my age and other conditions?  
- Are my current medications appropriate/titrated correctly (metoprolol 25 mg)? Should we add or switch meds (ACE inhibitor/ARB, calcium-channel blocker, thiazide diuretic) to reach goal?  
- Is my blood pressure well controlled on home readings? (I can bring a log.) Is ambulatory BP monitoring or home monitoring recommended?  
- Could my current meds interact with apixaban or affect my anemia or symptoms?  
- Do I need any new bloodwork before changing meds (BMP/creatinine, potassium, CBC, lipids, A1c, TSH)?  
- Should I have an ECG now? An echocardiogram? (If you have palpitations, syncope, or suspected structural disease)  
- Could there be secondary causes of hypertension we should evaluate (sleep apnea, thyroid disease, renal disease, hyperaldosteronism)?  
- Given my anticoagulation and history, is there any special blood pressure threshold for referral or further cardiac work-up?  
- Do you recommend lifestyle changes tailored to me (sodium, alcohol, weight, exercise) and specific follow-up interval?  
- At what point should we refer to cardiology?

When a cardiology referral is commonly recommended
- Hypertension that remains uncontrolled despite 3 medications including a diuretic (resistant HTN)  
- Evidence of cardiac damage (heart failure symptoms, markedly abnormal ECG, reduced ejection fraction, significant left ventricular hypertrophy)  
- Unexplained or recurrent arrhythmias, syncope, or ischemic symptoms  
- Need for specialized procedures or advanced testing (e.g., electrophysiology, complex imaging)  
- Younger patients with severe or secondary causes of hypertension may also warrant cardiology input — age 44 alone does not automatically require referral, but your overall risk factors and clinical picture matter.

Tests you may want to request or have available at the visit
- Recent labs: BMP/renal function, potassium, CBC (for anemia), lipid panel, A1c, TSH  
- ECG (resting) — often useful if on anticoagulation/possible AF  
- Consider ambulatory 24-hour BP monitoring if readings are inconsistent  
- Echocardiogram if symptoms or abnormal ECG or suspected structural heart disease  
