Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — this is a good time to prepare. A few clarifying questions first so I give the most useful, accurate suggestions:
- Can you confirm your current medications and doses (especially the one(s) for anemia)? You wrote metoprolol twice — is that a typo?
- Why are you taking apixaban (e.g., atrial fibrillation, prior DVT/PE, other)?
- When you said your last INR was 320, did you mean 3.20? (Note: INR is used to monitor warfarin, not apixaban.)

Assuming the meds and history above, here are focused things to think about and bring up with Dr. Wilson — and items that influence whether a cardiology referral is warranted.

What to track before the visit
- Home blood pressure log: morning and evening readings for 7–14 days, each taken after 5 minutes seated, arm at heart level. Bring the device brand and model.
- Symptoms: lightheadedness, dizziness, chest pain, palpitations, syncope, worsening shortness of breath, unusual bleeding or bruising.
- List of all meds, doses, OTCs, supplements (especially NSAIDs, herbal supplements).
- Family history of premature cardiovascular disease, sudden death, or inherited conditions.

Important labs and tests to request before or at the visit
- Basic metabolic panel (BMP) or comprehensive metabolic panel — check kidney function (eGFR) for apixaban dosing.
- CBC (for anemia status).
- Lipid panel.
- HbA1c (if diabetes risk).
- TSH (thyroid can affect BP and heart rhythm).
- ECG (to check rhythm — especially important if apixaban is for AF).
- If symptoms or abnormal findings: echocardiogram.

Questions to ask about hypertension management
- What is my individualized BP target, given my age and other conditions?
- Is my current regimen (metoprolol 25 mg) optimal, or should we adjust dose or add a different class (ACEi/ARB, calcium channel blocker, thiazide diuretic)?
- If my BP isn’t controlled on 2–3 meds, what is the plan (add diuretic, increase doses, check for secondary causes)?
- Could any of my other meds or supplements be affecting BP?
- Should I monitor orthostatic BPs or do home ambulatory monitoring?
- What lifestyle changes are most important for me (sodium intake, weight, exercise, alcohol)?

Specific considerations given apixaban, anemia, and depression
- Apixaban: dosing depends on indication and kidney function. Apixaban does not use INR for monitoring; if you have an INR value it suggests prior/other therapy — clarify that. Also ask about bleeding risk and whether any additional precautions are needed for procedures or labs.
- Anemia: identify cause (iron deficiency, chronic disease, etc.). Severe anemia can affect heart function and BP — ask that the PCP review recent CBC and plan.
- Depression and meds: some antidepressants can affect BP or interact with other meds — list them and ask about interactions.

When to consider referral to cardiology
- Known or new atrial fibrillation, symptomatic arrhythmia, or unclear indication for apixaban.
- Structural heart disease on exam/ECG/echo (valvular disease, cardiomyopathy).
- Resistant hypertension (BP uncontrolled on ≥3 medications including a diuretic).
- Recurrent syncope, suspicious palpitations, chest pain, or ischemic changes on ECG.
- Young age with early-onset cardiovascular disease or complex anticoagulation/arrhythmia management questions.
If you have AF or structural heart disease, a cardiology referral is reasonable. If your BP is controllable and there are no cardiac symptoms or ECG/echo abnormalities, your PCP may manage it.

Suggested phrasing to ask Dr. Wilson (pick the ones you like)
- “My BP readings at home average X/X over the past 2 weeks. What should my goal BP be, and do we need to change my meds?”
- “I’m taking apixaban for [reason]. Do you think I should see cardiology to review rhythm management or anticoagulation strategy?”
- “My last CBC/INR reads [values]. Can we review the anemia workup and whether it affects my cardiac risk or BP meds?”
- “Should I get an ECG or echocardiogram before seeing cardiology?”

Pre-visit checklist to bring to the appointment
- Printed home BP log.
- Current med list (including supplements).
- Symptoms list and questions prioritized.
- Recent labs or request that lab work be ordered ahead of the appointment (BMP, CBC, lipid, A1c, TSH) and an ECG.

Red flags (seek urgent care or call your PCP/ER)
- New chest pain, sudden severe shortness of breath, fainting, sudden weakness, slurred speech.
- Uncontrolled bleeding, black/tarry stools, coughing or vomiting blood, or very heavy bruising.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short message/email you can send to Dr. Wilson summarizing these points and asking whether to order labs/ECG and whether cardiology referral is recommended.
- Create a one-page checklist/questions list you can print and bring to the visit.

Tell me how you want to proceed and confirm those clarifying details (meds, reason for apixaban, INR number) and I’ll draft the message or checklist.

