I can help — this is a good time to prepare. A couple quick clarifying questions before I make suggestions and draft anything for you:

- Did you mean your last INR was 3.20 (not 320)? Also: INR is used for warfarin monitoring; you listed apixaban (a DOAC) — are you currently on apixaban only, or were you on warfarin previously?
- Is your metoprolol 25 mg daily or twice daily?
- Are you taking any medications or supplements for anemia (iron, etc.)?
- Do you have a history of atrial fibrillation, coronary artery disease, heart failure, stroke, or prior cardiac testing?

Once you confirm, I can tailor things more precisely. Meanwhile, here are general, practical questions and items to take to your annual physical with Dr. James Wilson — focused on hypertension management and whether you may need cardiology referral.

Suggested opening questions for the visit
- “What is my blood pressure target given my age (44) and medical history (on apixaban, metoprolol, anemia, history of depression)?”
- “Is my current regimen (metoprolol 25 mg [clarify frequency]) adequate, or should we adjust or add an antihypertensive?”
- “Could any of my current medications (apixaban, metoprolol, antidepressants, supplements) be affecting my blood pressure or interacting?”
- “Do you recommend home BP monitoring or ambulatory (24-hour) BP monitoring before we change meds?”
- “Are any additional tests indicated now (labs, imaging, cardiac testing) because of my risk factors?”

Specific follow-up/diagnostic tests to discuss
- Basic labs: BMP/CMP (kidney function, electrolytes), CBC (anemia), fasting lipids, HbA1c, TSH.
- Urinalysis (proteinuria) or urine albumin/creatinine ratio to assess kidney-related BP risk.
- EKG to check rhythm/incomplete conduction or prior atrial fibrillation.
- Echocardiogram if there’s concern for heart structure/function (LV hypertrophy, prior CAD symptoms).
- Ambulatory BP monitoring if there’s concern about white coat or masked hypertension or to confirm uncontrolled hypertension.
- If on warfarin previously or if INR was measured, clarify anticoagulant monitoring history.

Questions about medication strategy and safety
- “Should we change to a different class (ACE inhibitor/ARB, calcium channel blocker, thiazide) given my other meds and health conditions?”
- “Does being on apixaban change how aggressively we need to lower blood pressure (targets, bleeding risk)?”
- “Are there side effects of metoprolol I should watch for (fatigue, low HR) and do we need to check heart rate targets?”
- “Could my anemia or antidepressant treatment influence blood pressure or medication choices?”

When to consider cardiology referral (ask Dr. Wilson if any apply to you)
- Persistent uncontrolled BP despite 2–3 antihypertensives at appropriate doses (resistant hypertension).
- Suspicion of secondary causes of hypertension (early onset, sudden worsening, very high readings, low potassium, renal artery disease).
- Symptoms such as chest pain, syncope, persistent palpitations, or evidence of heart failure.
- Need for specialized testing (echocardiogram, advanced rhythm monitoring) or procedures.
- Complex anticoagulation + cardiac disease management questions (for example, if you have or are suspected to have atrial fibrillation or structural heart disease).

Red flags that warrant more urgent evaluation
- Systolic BP >180 or diastolic >120 with symptoms (headache, vision changes, chest pain, shortness of breath) — urgent/ER evaluation.
- New fainting, chest pain, sudden severe headache, or neurologic symptoms.

Practical items to bring or prepare
- A printed list of all medications (including doses and frequency) and supplements.
- Your home BP log for the last 1–4 weeks (times and readings).
- Any recent labs or documentation of INR if that was measured previously.
- A short summary of symptoms (dizziness, palpitations, chest pain, syncope, exercise tolerance).

Suggested phrasing if you want Dr. Wilson to decide about referral
- “Given my age and history (on apixaban, metoprolol, anemia, depression), do you think a referral to Colorado Cardiology would be helpful? If so, could you refer me for (evaluation for resistant HTN / echocardiogram / rhythm evaluation)?”
I can draft that as an email or message if you’d like.

Would you like me to:
- Draft an appointment checklist / conversation script for you to bring to the visit? OR
- Draft an email to Dr. Wilson asking specific questions and requesting a referral to Colorado Cardiology (I can include the clarifying points about INR and current meds)?

Tell me which and confirm the medication/INR details and I’ll prepare it.

